nodecode-gowa is a rewrite of anpicasso's anpicasso/hermes-go-whatsapp:

  https://github.com/anpicasso/hermes-go-whatsapp
  commit a48546353fe79456bc193402905b17b3e1cd1be6
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Angello Picasso (LICENSE)

The original registered three Hermes tools over one GOWA server -- gowa_devices
(list or inspect a device slot), gowa_device_login (pair one from a QR or a
phone code, creating the slot when asked) and gowa_device_remove (purge one
after an explicit confirmation) -- speaking to the server's REST API at
GET /devices, GET /devices/{id}, POST /devices, GET /devices/{id}/login,
POST /devices/{id}/login/code?phone= and DELETE /devices/{id}, with a whole
Basic or Bearer value the operator supplies. This cell keeps those three
tools, their names, their arguments and their refusals: a slot that is
already linked or pairing is not paired again, a number linked elsewhere is
named, create_if_missing alone makes a slot, a removal needs confirm, the
QR link must be on the server's own origin, and a base_url on plain HTTP is
loopback-only unless the section allows more. Every call is reimplemented in
Common Lisp against NLK:HTTP, and the original's JSON envelopes are answered
as the prose a model reads, with the QR written 0600 under the home.

What was left out: the GOWA installer. The original's `hermes gowa setup`
downloaded a pinned v9.5.0 release, verified its SHA-256, unpacked it under
the home, generated a Basic credential and a webhook secret, wrote a systemd
unit, a launchd agent or a scheduled task, and configured the host's MCP
server entry. A cell loads inside a running organism rather than beside a
CLI, so this one talks to a server the operator already runs, and /gowa is
the list the CLI's setup used to end at. Also left out: the Hermes plugin
manifest, its toolset wiring, and the config writes into another app's file.
